AB  - Soil erosion and sediment load into the Great Barrier Reef has increased in the last century, mainly due to vegetation clearing. The increased sediment load reduces light availability for marine ecosystems, can smother corals and seagrasses, increases the nutrient influx and can damage fish gills. While LiDAR data are often used to map erosion hotspots and guide remediation efforts, it is not possible to deploy across all catchments draining into the Great Barrier Reef (GBR) due to the large spatial scale. Here, we test the feasibility of Sentinel-1 (C-Band) and ALOS-2 ScanSAR (L-Band) data using coherence change detection, which maps anomalous InSAR coherence loss during major erosion events, for soil erosion mapping at the scale of the entire GBR catchment.
