AB  - Be-10 concentrations of six soils on the raised coral reef terraces of Kikai Island, southwest Japan range from 0.80 to 7.17 x 10(9) atoms g(-1). The annual deposition rate of Be-10 from the atmosphere to Kikai Island from 2000 to 2002 ranges from 2.0 to 3.5 x 10(6) atoms cm(-2) year(-1), with a mean of 2.8 ± 0.7 x 10(6) atoms cm(-2) year(-1). Minimum absolute ages, calculated from the inventory of meteoric Be-10 in the soils and the annual deposition rate of Be-10, are: Initial Rendzina-like soil, ≥ 8 ka; Rendzina-like soil, ≥ 18 ka; Brown Rendzina-like soil, ≥ 56 ka; Terra fusca-like soil, ≥ 127 ka; Terra rossa-like soil, ≥ 136 ka; and Intergrade between Terra rossa-like and Red-Yellow soil, ≥ 102 ka. Therefore, it was concluded that soil formation during the first ∼ 60,000 years would be as Rendzina-like soils and more than ∼ 100,000 years would be required for Red-Yellow soils to develop on raised coral reef terraces under subtropical humid climatic conditions. © 2004 Elsevier B.V. All rights resserved.
