KW  - EXTRACELLULAR POLYMERIC SUBSTANCES
KW  - SLUDGE
KW  - DEPOSITION
KW  - EPS
AB  - Reclaimed water reuse has become increasingly important in water-scarce regions, yet bioactive quorum sensing (QS) signals that may occur in wastewater-related systems could influence microbial transport during subsurface infiltration. Here, we investigated how environmentally relevant concentrations of N-acyl homoserine lactones (AHLs) regulate Escherichia coli (E. coli) transport in carbonate porous media using coral sand as a representative matrix. Column experiments showed that AHLs enhanced breakthrough in a non-monotonic manner, with maximum promotion at 100 ng L- 1. At this concentration, deposition rate coefficients decreased by up to 51%, and the enhancement persisted across varying grain sizes, pore-water velocities, ionic strengths, and complex water matrices (reclaimed water, lake water, and humic acid-amended solutions). Exposure to AHLs did not increase motility or induce biofilm-mediated pore clogging over experimental timescales. Instead, QS activation reprogrammed extracellular polymeric substances by reducing the protein-to-polysaccharide ratio, increasing surface negativity, and weakening adhesion. Derjaguin-Landau-Verwey-Overbeek (DLVO) analysis and density functional theory calculations further indicated preferential interactions between carbonate surfaces and proteinaceous extracellular polymeric substances (EPS) components, explaining the reduced attachment. These findings suggest that QS signals introduced via reclaimed water may enhance microbial mobility in carbonatedominated subsurface systems, with implications for groundwater protection and subsurface water reuse management.
