AB  - Most dive-based monitoring methods to assess sedimentation impacts to coral reefs resulting from dredging have been informative but limited in scope. Sponges and octocorals are typically not surveyed for sediment stress, and the time-consuming nature of surveys and data analysis has limited their utility for rapid decision-making during dredging operations. Here, we developed a rapid dive-based survey method to document spatiotemporal trends in reef-habitat sediment deposition and prevalence of sediment stress indicators in benthic taxa (stony corals, octocorals, and sponges) at two commercially-important ports in southeast Florida with planned expansions in the near future (Port Everglades and Port Miami). Across years, we found low levels of sediment deposition on coral reef habitats surrounding Port Everglades, and corresponding low sediment stress on benthic organisms, results expected for a location that has not experienced a recent port expansion or major sediment disturbance event. At Port Miami, however, we found higher sediment accumulation, higher proportion of fine-grained sediments, and associated sediment stress indicators in all benthic taxa, consistent with impacts reported from previous dredging operations. In addition, distinct taxon-specific stress patterns were observed across different reef habitats and orientation with respect to the port channel. These findings suggest that sediment depth measurements, combined with observations of indicators of stress on benthic taxa, can be a valuable tool for rapidly assessing potential dredge-related impacts on coral habitats over time. These tools can inform the adaptive management of dredging operations, and development of effective monitoring plans and corrective actions to minimize impacts to coral reef habitats.
