AB  - An exceptional fossil from the Famennian (Upper Devonian) of Pennsylvania preserves a monobathrid crinoid inside a hexactinellid sponge (Calathospongia sp.), providing potential evidence of commensalism. The specimen was likely collected from the lower Knapp Formation, deposited just prior to the end-Devonian mass extinction. Given their close association in death, it is plausible that the crinoid was attached to the vase-shaped sponge while alive, although we cannot completely exclude the possibility that it washed in during sediment deposition. An ophiuroid was previously described preserved within Calathospongia from a nearby locality, bolstering the case that echinoderms and hexactinellids lived in association during the Late Devonian in the Appalachian Basin. Many modern species, including crinoids and ophiuroids, live in or on sponges, and these relationships probably had a long geological history. An association of echinoderms and sponges in the Late Devonian might reflect trends toward increased predator avoidance and/or tiering height in marine communities during the Paleozoic.
