AB  - The construction of subsurface structures in offshore island reefs is critical for marine resource exploitation and logistical support. However, structural stability faces severe challenges. High-pressure seawater and porous coral limestone create complex hydro-mechanical coupling. This coupling is particularly dangerous under seismic excitation. This study investigates dynamic stability under these coupled seismic-hydrodynamic conditions. We developed a fluid-solid coupled model using the Discrete Element Method (DEM). A Voronoi-based stochastic pore generation technique was adopted. This accurately replicates the heterogeneous, vuggy porosity of reef limestone. Systematic simulations examined the dynamic responses of these subsurface structures. Variables included burial depths, spans, seaward slope gradients, and seismic accelerations. Results reveal a critical seismic acceleration threshold of 0.100 g. Exceeding this limit triggers a damage-dominated state. In this state, dynamic stress and pore pressure coupling exacerbate crack propagation. For geometric parameters, maintaining specific lateral cover is essential for rock integrity. This thickness must exceed 2.3 times the chamber span (2.3D). Sensitivity analysis shows seismic acceleration is the primary stability factor, followed by burial depth and span. Slope gradient mainly influences failure zone directionality. These findings provide essential guidelines for offshore reef infrastructure design.
