T2  - SEDIMENTOLOGY
LA  - English
AB  - Campanian (Upper Cretaceous) Chalk exposed in a quarry near Portsmouth is unusual in the occurrence of a contemporaneous anticlinal fold, probably diapiric in origin. Hardgrounds and local slumps developed over the structure, and part of the chalk succession thins considerably over its crest. Erosional channels, sometimes containing bioclastic lag deposits, formed at 2 levels. Movement on the anticline occurred 3 times during the part of the Lower Campanian represented in the section; the 3rd phase resulted in extensive fracturing of the crestal region of the fold. Burial of the structure and the onset of normal chalk deposition concluded the Cretaceous phase of its history. [The associated fauna is abundant including heteromorph ammonites, nautiloides, gastropods, scaphopods, bivalves and corals.].
