KW  - PALEOZOIC ICE-AGE
KW  - CARBONIFEROUS BOUNDARY
KW  - OXYGEN ISOTOPES
KW  - ATMOSPHERIC CO2
KW  - MONTAGNE-NOIRE
KW  - LEAF EVOLUTION
KW  - CARBON BURIAL
KW  - LAND PLANTS
KW  - CLIMATE
KW  - EXTINCTION
AB  - The origin and flourishing of Palaeozoic terrestrial plants led to prominent changes in the hydrologic cycle, which brought profound influences on the Earth surface system. First, plants can bring rainfall on Earth's surface through evaportranspiration, which increases the terrestrial silicate weathering rate and organic carbon burial volume. On the other hand, the nutrients released from weathering can be transported into marine environments by rivers, resulting in elevation of marine primary production and increases in marine anoxic condition and organic burial rate. These hydrologic cycle processes could have lowered atmosphere CO2 concentration and decreased seawater surface temperature. The Late Devonian-Mississippian period is a critical interval for the evolution of terrestrial plants and marine organic reefs, under the transition from greenhouse to icehouse climates. During this interval, the diversity, distribution and abundance of terrestrial plants obviously changed, which are characterized by (1) increases in the diversity and coverage with expansion to higher latitude of plants across the Late Devonian Frasnian-Famennian (F-F) transition; (2) the first appearance of seed plants in the middle Famennian and distinct increase in their diversity across the Devonian-Carboniferous (D-C) transition; and (3) the prominent development of cordaitalean trees with large height and deep roots during the Late Mississippian. The seed plants not only have deep roots but also can overcome dependence on aqueous environments, which permits their expansion from lower humid land to upper dry land. Thus, the occurrence of seed plants can significantly strengthen hydrologic cycle and cause severe effects on palaeoclimate and marine environments, which could have played an important role in regulating the evolution of marine faunas and reef ecosystems. From the Late Devonian to Mississippian, obvious changes in marine ecosystems occurred, including (1) the F-F Kellwasser mass extinction event accompanied with the collapse of the largest metazoan (stromatoporoid-coral) reef ecosystems of the Phanerozoic; (2) the D-C Hangenberg mass extinction event associated with the disappearance of stromatoporoid-coral reefs, followed by a metazoan "reef gap" in the Tournaisian Stage (Early Mississippian); and (3) the recovery and proliferation of coral reef ecosystems during the late Visean Stage (Middle Mississippian) which collapsed during the Middle-Late Mississippian mass extinction event. According to comparative studies on changes in terrestrial plants and marine reefs, the collapse and disappearance of marine metazoan reefs are closely related to the increases in terrestrial silicate weathering rate and hydrologic cycle intensity under plant proliferation, especially during mass extinction transitions. The latter could have induced the decrease in seawater temperature and increase in anoxic conditions, which probably are the main controlling factors on metazoan reef evolution during this time. This hypothesis is supported by the occurrence of glacial deposits on the Gondwana Continent and/or black shales in the marine, positive shift in conodont/brachiopod oxygen isotopes and negative shift in uranium isotopes during the F-F, D-C and M-LM transitions. In addition, the orogeny with terrestrial uplift and volcanism can enhance silicate weathering, which can further trigger deterioration in marine environments and collapse of marine ecosystems.
   To date, the precise correlation of the evolution between terrestrial plants and marine organic reefs is still hampered due to the low-resolution stratigraphic framework for the terrestrial realm. More studies are required in the future, including (1) reconstruct high-resolution stratigraphy and plant evolutionary patterns, (2) use geochemical proxies to constrain the relationship between terrestrial plant proliferation, weathering and marine anoxic conditions, and (3) develop modeling to provide quantitative evaluation on the influence of plant hydrologic cycle on terrestrial weathering and input. These works would provide new insights into the co-evolution between terrestrial and marine ecosystems during the critical transition from the Late Devonian to Mississippian.
