AB  - A series of surface and buried explosion tests with varying explosive equivalents, burial depths, and types of sand were conducted to investigate the propagation and attenuation characteristics of blast waves. The shock wave velocity and rise time were derived from the normal stress time-history curves. It was found that in calcareous sand, the rise time increases with scaled distance, with an accelerating rate as the shock wave transitions into a compression wave, whereas no significant variation is observed in silica sand. The ratio of rise time to arrival time in calcareous sand follows a slowly growing exponential function with scaled distance. The mechanisms of blast wave propagation, including parametric analyses and predictions, were investigated using a GPUaccelerated Smoothed Particle Hydrodynamics (SPH) program that incorporates a compressive rate-dependent hypoplastic constitutive (CRDHC) model, all utilizing the same set of parameters. The propagation and attenuation law of peak stress was established based on experimental and numerical results, with predicted normal stress time-history curves showing excellent agreement with measurements. The calcareous sand exhibits only 20%-30% of the peak stress observed in silica sand because its high porosity and crushability markedly enhance stress wave attenuation, rendering it superior for protective engineering applications.
