TI  - THE SUPRATIDAL DEPOSITS AND EFFECTS OF CORAL DREDGING ON MUD ISLAND, MORETON BAY, SOUTHEAST QUEENSLAND
T2  - ZEITSCHRIFT FUR GEOMORPHOLOGIE
LA  - English
AB  - Prior to coral dredging Mud Island was a 1200 ha coral reef island. Landforms consisted of a seaward sloping open reef flat with no reef rim, which surrounded an inner reef flat area containing a mangrove park with creeks, swales, 21 sand islets formed during the late Holocene, and a central continental island core. The islets are composed of poorly-sorted coarse sands, range in elevation from 0.5 to 2.0 m, and are colonised predominantly by dry littoral woodland with mangroves on their margins. The geomorphic characteristics of Mud Island classify it as a ''mangrove island'', a type of coral reef island found in low energy environments at low latitudes. Consequently, the latitudinal range over which mangrove islands are known to occur is doubled. Air photo analysis shows that coral dredging for cement manufacture has removed about 74% of the open reef flat which is 50% of the island's total area. An inventory of coral dredging has been constructed using aerial photographic interpretation, bathymetric maps, and anecdotal evidence of dredgemasters. Dredging over a 54 year period has mobilised coral boulders and formed shingle ridges with moderately-sorted supratidal pebble-sized sediment crests which have accumulated on the open reef flat. The ridges have entered parts of the inner reef flat and destroyed mangroves by burial and abrasion. Ridge area increased by 62% from 1972 to 1991, and the ridges now encompass 75% of the perimeter of the mangrove park. The ridges have transgressed the open reef flat at an average rate of 2.5 m.yr(-1). The transgression rate has decreased over time. The shingle ridges have restricted tidal flushing of the mangrove park killing mangroves near creek mouths by waterlogging. Creeks and swales have expanded in area by 52% from 1958 to 1991.
