AB  - The Hoca Hasan Bathhouse is located in Harput, the historic settlement of Elazig. The bathhouse, which is owned by the Elazig Municipality, was officially registered by the relevant council in 1985. In 2016, cleaning operations and trial excavations were carried out under the supervision of the Elazig Museum as part of the restoration project. The restoration plans were approved by the relevant council in 2017, and restoration work on the structure commenced in 2021. During the implementation phase, excavation and cleaning works were resumed. In the course of these excavations, it was determined that the bathhouse whose project had been approved as a four-iwan (four-cornered) scheme with private halvet cells arranged around the hot chamber extended further to the northwest. In addition to the four halvet cells, new associated units were identified, including an additional halvet room and raised platforms (sekis) used for cooling. It was observed that the east, west, and north walls of this newly identified halvet cell to the northeast were clad with tiles up to approximately 150 cm above floor level. The tile panels on the walls were found to be framed by rectangular borders at the lower and upper sections, and that the main composition was formed through the orderly arrangement of principal panels of consistent dimensions. Although a total of 105 tile panels should be present on the three walls according to the design scheme, only a limited number of principal panels and borders, along with numerous fragments, were recovered during the excavations. The body of the tiles is approximately 1.5 cm thick, finely sieved, and densely textured; its color is defined as very pale brown according to the Munsell catalog. A pebble-containing mortar was used on the reverse surfaces of the tiles to affix them to the wall. In addition to blue-and-white, green, and black, the color palette includes shades of red characteristic of the Ottoman classical period; however, the red used here is closer to pale, brick-like tones rather than the distinctive coral red. This suggests features indicative of local production. Furthermore, fading of colors, pattern bleeding, and extensive glaze cracking were observed on the tiles. These characteristics are associated both with the quality of production and with their prolonged burial in damp soil. When the tile revetments in Hoca Hasan Bathhouse are examined in terms of motif and composition, a compositional approach dominated by vegetal ornamentation is observed. The flowers depicted in tile art are, to a great extent, stylized representations of plants found in nature, prepared as designs in the Imperial Nakkashane (court atelier), and widely applied across various artistic media, as evidenced in numerous works. The motifs used on the tiles hatayi, tulip, carnation, hyacinth, daisy, rumi, dagger (hancer), central medallion (ortabag), and interlacing scrolling branches are arranged in an intertwined manner, forming a naturalistic composition. The bathhouse and its tiles examined in this study are of considerable significance, as they constitute the first example in the region of a tiled bath in which the tiles have been identified in situ. This study aims, on the one hand, to redefine and evaluate the bathhouse from an architectural perspective and, on the other, to establish the place and significance of the tile finds within the field of Art History.
