TI  - ORIGIN OF DOLOMITES IN A DOWNSLOPE BIOSTROME, JEFFERSON FORMATION (DEVONIAN), CENTRAL IDAHO - EVIDENCE FROM REE PATTERNS, STABLE ISOTOPES, AND PETROGRAPHY
T2  - BULLETIN OF CANADIAN PETROLEUM GEOLOGY
LA  - English
AB  - A completely dolomitized coral-stromatoporoid biostrome occurs at the top of the Dark Dolomite Member of the Jefferson Formation (Frasnian) at Grandview Canyon, Lost River Range, east-central Idaho. The biostrome overlies a thick sequence of dolostones that were deposited in slope to deep ramp settings. The biostrome probably formed in an open marine setting during regional shallowing of deep water environments. Several episodes of dolomitization or dolomite recrystallization probably affected  the biostrome. Dolomite types can be subdivided according to their cathodoluminescent characteristics: homogeneous dully luminescent replacive dolomite (most common dolomite type), "irregularly" luminescent replacive dolomite, and zoned luminescent dolospar cement. Homogeneous daily luminescent dolomite is not zoned under cathodoluminescence. Irregularly luminescent dolomite is dully luminescent, with abundant 1-10 .mu.m irregular patches of nonluminescent and more brightly luminescent dolomite.  The irregular patches are interpreted as microdissolution or recrystallization fabrics and probably represent incomplete replacement of early dolomite phases by later dolomite phases. Microprobe and X-ray diffraction analyses indicate that both replacive dolomite types (as well as dolospar cements) are nearly stoichiometric (Ca48.5 Mg51.5(CO3)2 to Ca51 Mg49(CO3)2). Stable isotopes from homogeneous dull and irregularly luminescent dolomites are: .delta.18O = -7.2 to -4.7.permill. (ave. -5.8.permill.) and .delta.13C = 0 to -0.4.permill. (ave. -0.2.permill.), relative to PDB. Chondrite-normalized REE (rare earth element) patterns from homogeneous dull and irregularly luminescent dolomites are very similar. They show negative Ce and Eu anomalies and light REE enrichment, which are similar to chondrite-normalized REE patterns for modern seawater. Additionally, the Ce anomaly becomes more negative upward through the biostrome as an erosional surface, which separates the biostrome and the overlying Grandview Dolomite, is approached. Coarse crystalline zoned dolospar fills dissolution vugs and tectonic fractures and it is also nearly stoichiometric. The same cathodoluminescent zonation is observed in all coarse dolospar throughout the biostrome. Stable isotopes for zoned dolospar are: .delta.18O = -13.1 to -10.3.permill. (ave. -12.6.permill.) and .delta.13C = 01.6 -0.1.permill. (ave. -0.4.permill.). Chondrite-normalized REE patterns for zoned dolospar are different from REE patterns for the replacive dolomites in that they have positive Ce anomalies and no significant Eu anomalies. Stratigraphic occurrence in an open marine setting, timing of dolomitization, stable isotopes, and REE patterns suggest homogeneous dull and irregularly luminescent dolomite formed during shallow burial diagenesis with significant involvement of nonevaporated seawater. More negative Ce anomalies in replacive dolomites that are stratigraphically nearer to the erosional surface on top of the biostrome suggest that  oxidizing meteoric waters may have caused recrystallization of these dolomites. Occurrence in dissolution vugs and fractures, and light oxygen isotopes suggest zoned dolospar formed from warm fluids. Carbon for zoned dolospar probably was recycled from preexisting dolomite, as is indicated by the close similarity of carbon isotopes for all dolomite phases. Positive Ce anomalies and lack of significant negative Eu anomalies suggest that dolospar-precipitating fluids were reducing and may have  derived some of their REE from shale or igneous sources. Hydrothermal fluids also may account for the light oxygen isotopes and positive Ce anomalies in the vug- and fracture-filling dolospar cements.
