TI  - EFFECTS OF A PHOSPHATE SHIP GROUNDING ON A RED-SEA CORAL-REEF
T2  - MARINE POLLUTION BULLETIN
LA  - English
KW  - AQABA
KW  - GULF
AB  - The ship Safir, carrying a cargo of phosphate (crushed fluorapatite), ran aground at Ras Nasrani in the northern Red Sea in 1989. This study documents damage to the coral reef resulting from the impact and release of several hundred tonnes of phosphate. Damage was severe but localized with benthic life being virtually eliminated within approximately 500 m2 of reef. This was primarily due to crushing by the ship and smothering by phosphate powder. Levels of dissolved phosphate at the site were elevated following the accident suggesting the possibility of an algal bloom due to nutrient enrichment. However, no macro-algal bloom was recorded. Adverse effects did not spread beyond the area of impact, and by seven months after the accident there were signs of recovery. However, recovery is likely to be inhibited by the large amount of phosphate powder remaining as sediment, which may be expected to inhibit coral settlement and possibly reduce growth.
