T2  - MARINE BIOLOGY
LA  - English
AB  - Environmental variables (14) were monitored at 7 locations along the west coast of Barbados on a weekly basis over a 1-yr period, 1981-1982. The physicochemical and biological data indicate that an environmental gradient exists as a result of increased eutrophication of coastal waters. Growth rates (linear extension) of M. annularis (Ellis and Solander), measured along the environmental gradient, exhibit high correlation with a number of water quality variables. Concentration of suspended particulate matter is the best univariate estimator of M. annularis skeletal extension rates (r2 = 0.79, P < 0.0001). Suspended particulate matter may be an energy source for reef corals, increasing growth up to a certain maximum concentration. After this, reduction of growth occurs due to smothering, reduced light levels and reduced zooxanthellae photosynthesis.
