TI  - IMPACT OF A KAOLIN CLAY SPILL ON A CORAL-REEF IN HAWAII
T2  - MARINE BIOLOGY
LA  - English
AB  - On 27 April, 1980, the Greek frieghter Anangel Liberty went aground on the reef at French Frigate Shoals, a National Wildlife Refuge in the Hawaiian Islands. The vessel was refloated with no major damage or fuel spillage after 2220 tons (2,200,000 kg) of koalin cargo had been jettisoned on the reef. Huge plumes of suspended clay raised major concern over the possiblity of widespread ecological damage. Field investigations conducted 14 days after the kaolin was dumped revealed that environmental impact was minor and highly localized; most kaolin had been suspended and removed from the area. The only significant damage was a 2-3 m deep channel plowed through the reef by the freigher. Within 50 m of both sides of the channel, some coral was smothered and colonies of Pocillopora spp. were alive but slightly bleached. Beyond 50 m there was no apparent impact or clay settling on the bottom. This incident illustrated that some events which initially appeared to have potential pollutant impact did not produce significant and irreversible environemntal changes and emphasized the need to analyze such events on a case-by-case basis.
